            <ArticleTitle>The beta-adrenergic signaling pathway in the heart.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>43-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Postsynaptic defects in this important pathway for regulating heart rate and contractile force may contribute to the etiology and pathogenesis of congestive cardiac failure. Such defects may involve the beta-adrenergic receptor, its associated G protein, or adenylate cyclase. Current knowledge about these components of transmembrane cardiac signaling is reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
